{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filename": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filepath": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "page": 1, "title": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "year": "2026"}, "page_content": "Understanding Topical Treatments\n\nTopical treatments are medications applied directly to the skin. They are often the first line of treatment for mild to moderate psoriasis, and can also be used in conjunction with systemic treatments for more severe cases. The goal of topical therapy is to reduce inflammation, slow the rapid growth of skin cells, and alleviate symptoms such as itching and scaling.\n\nTypes of Topical Treatments\n\nCorticosteroids:\u00a0Available in various strengths, corticosteroids reduce inflammation and slow the production of skin cells. Commonly prescribed corticosteroids include betamethasone and hydrocortisone.\n\nVitamin D analogues:\u00a0These synthetic forms of vitamin D, such as calcipotriol, help to slow down skin cell production and can be used alone or in combination with corticosteroids.\n\nCoal tar:\u00a0An older treatment, coal tar helps to reduce scaling, itching, and inflammation. It is available in shampoos, creams, and bath solutions.\n\nSalicylic acid:\u00a0Salicylic acid is a beta-hydroxy acid (BHA) that has been used for decades in the treatment of various skin conditions, including psoriasis.", "type": "Document"}}